poker on OS-9 -- what was changed=================================Five card draw poker against the computer, by Nic Smith, written as afinal-year project on BSD 4.2 (DYNIX).  comp.sources.games volume 7,issues 18 and 19 (July 1989), with Patch1 (volume 7, issue 41), whichwas a new release from the author plus a set of #ifdefs for HP-UX onthe series 300.  The posting carries no copyright or licence notice.ORIG/ holds the posting's twelve files as the two shar parts extractedthem, and patches01, the context diff that Patch1 carried.  The sourceshere are those files with patches01 applied, and then the changes below,every one of them inside #ifdef OSK.  Nothing in the game's play, itsstrategy table or its screen layout was changed.THE MODULE IS NAMED drawpoker.  CMDS/GAMES/poker on this disk is adifferent game, the SNOBOL4-in-C cold-hand poker, and OS-9 finds aresident module by name, so two modules called poker would be oneprogram.  The recipe's -n=drawpoker names it; nothing in the sourcerefers to its own name.  The rules file is the copy this disk alreadycarried at /dd/GAMES/LIB/poker_rules (tabs expanded, otherwise thesame as the one here).1.  THE RULES ARE PAGED WITH less, FORKED DIRECTLY.  The original built    the command line "/usr/ucb/more /usr/games/lib/poker_rules" and handed    it to system().  Microware C's system() forks a module named `shell',    which a system need not have resident, so p_scr.c now has a small    page_rules() that forks the pager with os9exec(os9fork, ...) and    waits for it.  poker.h names the pager (PAGER) and the file (RULES):        #define PAGER "less"        #define RULES "/dd/GAMES/LIB/poker_rules"    less rather than more because more on this disk reads its answer as    a line -- every page waits for Enter -- while less takes the single    key the rules page asks for, and it shows the rules' backspace    underlining as underlining.  poker_rules itself is unchanged apart    from its line ends.2.  THE MESSAGE PAUSES SLEEP INSTEAD OF COUNTING.  Six places held a    message on the screen with an empty loop to 900000 (PAUSE_LEN),    which the author tuned for a Sequent and warned would need changing    on other machines.  Under OSK each is tsleep(PAUSE_TICKS), about one    and a half seconds (384/256ths), whatever the processor.  Without    it, "I will take 2 cards." and "We ante." were erased before they    could be read.3.  THE SHUFFLE IS SEEDED FROM THE CLOCK AS WELL AS THE PROCESS ID.    srand(getpid()) alone gave the same deal every time the program was    started in the same place in a session.  It is now    srand(time(0) ^ getpid()).4.  CONTROL-R REPAINTS THE SCREEN.  The original's wrefresh(curscr)    repaints everything in BSD curses; OS-9's curses only does that when    curscr has been marked with clearok(), so redraw_screen() now does    that first.5.  exit() IS GIVEN A STATUS.  die() called exit() with no argument,    which left the program's exit status at whatever was in the register    -- 1, as measured, which a shell reports as an error.  It is exit(0).Build: cc -qm with curses.l and termlib.l, as recipes.psv has it.  Thefiles are compiled as posted apart from the above; the K&R code needednothing else.WHAT IT NEEDS TO RUN.  TERM and TERMCAP, which SYS/login sets.  Therules at /dd/GAMES/LIB/poker_rules and less in the executiondirectory, only if you answer y to "Do you want instructions andrules ?".KEYS.  Space leaves the title.  s, d, b to Stay, Drop or Bet; r, d, c toRaise, Drop or Call; 1 to 5 for the amount (5 to 25).  At "Which cardsdo you want to reject" type up to three card numbers and press Enter;Backspace starts the choice again.  y or n for another hand.  F asks"Do you really want to quit ?" at any prompt, and control-R repaints.CONTROL-C is left as the author wrote it: SIGINT and SIGQUIT are caughtand lead to the same quit question.  Under os9exec on a terminal thekeyboard interrupt did not arrive at all -- not at cat either -- so thatpath is untested here; F is the way out that was measured.